For an activation-triggering input event, Ladybird now activates the
window of the event’s document, those of its ancestor navigables, and
those of its same-origin descendant navigables — in other processes
too, by way of the UI process. WebKit does the same: LocalDOMWindow::
notifyActivated() activates the local ancestors and same-origin local
descendants, then sends didNotifyUserActivation() to the UI process
when the tree has a remote frame. Gecko follows the same steps, in its
Document::NotifyUserGestureActivation(), and returns early rather than
asserting when the document has no browsing context. Blink’s Frame::
NotifyUserActivationInFrameTree() activates the ancestors and every
same-origin frame of the tree, siblings included, which goes further
than the spec (see whatwg/html#9831).
